            <section data-transition="slide-in none-out">
                {# Problem statement #}
                <h2>Problem statement</h2>
                <div>
                    The problem that BrickFormer aims to solve, is to maximize the <b>similarity</b> between a LEGO construction $\mathcal{p}$ and a target 3D model $\mathcal{M}$.
                    The similarity is defined in terms of shape and color. The "LEGO construction" has a given resolution and is a combination of bricks drawn from a provided discrete set. Among the possible combinations some of them are not valid.
                </div>
                <div>
                    \[\begin{aligned}
                        p^* = \arg\max_{p \in \mathbb{P}} \operatorname{similarity}(p, \mathcal{M})
                    \end{aligned} \]
                </div>
            </section>

            <section data-transition="none">
                <h2 style="margin-bottom: 10px">Problem statement</h2>
                <h4 style="margin-top: 0">The similarity function</h4>
                <div>
                    We can trivially model a similarity score that will be higher if $p$ 
                    is close to the target model. Considering both live in the same 3D space, a measure of similarity could be provided by aggregating the structural distance, and color distance, of every individual brick $b$ to the closest point on the model.
                </div>
                <div>
                    \[\begin{aligned}
                        \operatorname{similarity}(p, \mathcal{M}) = \dfrac{1}{|p|} \sum_{b \in p} \left( \operatorname{dist}(b_{\text{position}}, \mathcal{M}) + \operatorname{dist}(b_{\text{color}}, \mathcal{M}) \right)
                    \end{aligned} \]
                </div>
                <div>
                    The closest point on the model could be efficiently retrieved through a spatial data structure (e.g. a KD-tree).
                </div>
            </section>

            <section data-transition="none-in slide-out">
                <h2 style="margin-bottom: 10px">Problem statement</h2>
                <h4 style="margin-top: 0">The solution space</h4>
                    <div>
                        <p>
                        To simplify the problem, the set of available bricks $\mathbb{B}$ is made of standard LEGO bricks (as shown below). Different rotations of these bricks are treated as different bricks. Placements locations are binned in voxels within a 3D grid defined by the input resolution $\mathcal{R}$.
                        Nonetheless these simplifications, the solution space $\mathbb{P}$ remains discrete and insanely large.
                        For every voxel, any combination of bricks could be placed, and of any color.
                        The cardinality would be:</p>
                        <div>
                            \[\begin{aligned}
                                |\mathbb{P}| = \left| \mathcal{R}_x \times \mathcal{R}_y \times \mathcal{R}_z \times \mathbb{B} \times \mathbb{C} \right|
                            \end{aligned} \]
                        </div>
                        <p>Where $\mathbb{C}$ is the set of available colors.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        Most notably, the solution space is populated with <b>invalid</b> constructions.
                        For example: overlapping bricks, floating bricks, bricks spanning out of the provided resolution.
                        Moreover, some constructions might share high similarity, but one should be preferred to another in terms of <b>stability</b>.
                    </div><br>
                    <div>
                        It's clear that addressing the problem globally is unfeasible, hence why I've opted for a <b>greedy solution</b>.</div>
                        <br>

