            {# ---------------------------------------------------------------- #}
            {# Greedy approach #}
            {# ---------------------------------------------------------------- #}

            {# Bottom-up, slice-by-slice construction #}
            <section data-transition="slide-in none-out">
                <h2 style="margin-bottom: 10px">The greedy algorithm</h2>
                <h4 style="margin-top: 0">Bottom-up, slice-by-slice construction</h4>
                <div>
                    In real life, LEGO constructions are built bottom-up, hence I decided having the tool doing the same.<br>
                    <br>
                    The model is first sliced into $\mathcal{R}_y$ <b>slices</b>,
                    and each slice is processed incrementally.
                    In this way we can strip the height dimension from the search space:
                    <div>
                        \[\begin{aligned}
                            | \hat{\mathbb{P}} | = \left| \mathcal{R}_x \times \mathcal{R}_z \times \mathbb{B} \times \mathbb{C} \right|
                        \end{aligned} \]
                    </div>

                    <div>
                    Putting in practical numbers, the number of available bricks is $|\mathbb{B}| = 22$, and the colors are $|\mathbb{C}| = 67$. If we constrain the resolution $\mathcal{R}_{\{x,z\}}$ to $\{1, ..., 150\}$, the worst case solution space would be:
                    
                    \[\begin{aligned}
                        150 \times 150 \times 22 \times 67 = 33165000
                    \end{aligned} \]

                    For each, we evaluate the similarity function,
                    leading to a final algorithm that would be already treatable by modern GPUs (possibly chunked in multiple kernel dispatches).<br>
                    <br>
                    However, we still didn't get rid of <b>invalid solutions</b>: some solutions could have overlapping bricks,
                    floating bricks or bricks spanning out of the conversion grid.
                    While checking if a brick is floating or out of bounds is simpler in the 2D case (and could be done when evaluating the similarity function), <u>checking if bricks of the same solution are overlapping is not trivial</u> as it requires a second pass and additional storage. 
                    </div>

                    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: center; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px; gap: 20px;">
